n of `users`.** `lower(u.email)` wraps the column in a function. A plain index on `email` can't be used, so the planner reads every row and lowercases each email. This is the main cost.\n2. **Possible full scan of `orders`.** The join key is `o.user_id`. If it has no index, each matching user means a scan of all of `orders`, or a hash join that reads all of `orders`. Postgres doesn't index foreign keys automatically, and MySQL only does so for FK constraints.\n3. **Extra I/O from `SELECT *`.** It pulls every column from both tables. That rules out index-only scans, widens the rows, and returns `id`, `user_id` and other columns twice. Clients that map rows by column name can break on the duplicates.\n\n## Fixes\n\n**1. Make the email lookup indexable.** Pick one:\n\n```sql\n-- Option A: expression index (Postgres). The query stays unchanged.\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));\n\n-- Option B: case-insensitive type (Postgres)\nALTER TABLE users ALTER COLUMN email TYPE citext;\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX users_email_idx ON users (email);\n-- then: WHERE u.email = 'a@example.org'\n```\n\nIn MySQL, the default collations are already case-insensitive. Drop `lower()` and use a plain index on `email`. In SQL Server, use a case-insensitive collation or a computed column with an index.\n\n**2. Index the join column.**\n\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX orders_user_id_idx ON orders (user_id);\n```\n\nIf you usually want the latest orders, use `(user_id, created_at DESC)` instead. That also avoids a sort.\n\n**3. Select only the columns you need.**\n\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.status, o.total, o.created_at, u.id AS user_id\nFROM orders o\nJOIN users u ON u.id = o.user_id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\n\nThe column names above are placeholders. I don't have the schema. If `orders` has a `created_at` column and the result set can be large, add `ORDER BY o.created_at DESC LIMIT n`.\n\n## What to expect\n\nWith both indexes, the plan should be an index lookup on `users` that returns one row. A nested-loop index lookup on `orders` follows. The cost then depends on how many orders that user has, not on the size of either table.\n\nI haven't run `EXPLAIN`, because there's no schema or database in the workspace. The analysis comes from reading the query only. Please confirm with `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` against your real data. Also check whether the indexes already exist, since I can't see that from here.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}